          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is a migraine, and how is it different from an ordinary headache?",
          "answer": "Migraine is a recurring neurological disorder. Headache is its most prominent feature, but it also involves sensitivity to light, sound and smells, and nausea. For most people it comes in episodes, though a minority have it chronically (*Migraine*, condition reference).\n\nA typical migraine attack:\n- Is often one-sided and pulsating, though it can affect both sides.\n- Ranges from moderate to severe.\n- Gets worse with routine physical activity.\n- Often comes with nausea, vomiting, or sensitivity to light and sound.\n- Lasts several hours to three days if untreated (*Migraine*, condition reference).\n\nMigraine is classed as a primary headache disorder, meaning the headache is the condition itself rather than a symptom of another illness. In the ICD-10-CM coding system, migraine sits in the G40–G47 block with other episodic headache disorders such as cluster and tension-type headache (*ICD-10-CM Chapter 6*). I could not find the specific migraine code in the material I searched, so check that with a clinician or coder.\n\nThe material I searched does not compare migraine with tension-type headache in detail, so I cannot give a full side-by-side. A clinician is the right person to work out which kind of headache someone has.\n\nSome headaches need urgent care. Seek emergency help straight away for a sudden, severe headache that is the worst of your life, or for a headache with new neurological symptoms, confusion, or fever with a stiff neck (*Sudden severe headache and other dangerous headache patterns*).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
